  "body": {
    "kind": "theorem",
    "statement": "The adaptive-calibration question has an affirmative answer in its literal weak \\emph{no wider} sense. For every \\(n\\geq3\\), the spectral-split handle \\(\\mathrm{CalHandle}\\) admits the measurable constant covariance envelope \\(\\widehat K_{\\Sigma,n}\\equiv K_\\Sigma\\) and the choices\n\\[\n \\widetilde{\\mathcal C}_n^M(\\alpha)=\\mathcal C_n^M(\\alpha),\n \\qquad\n \\widetilde{\\mathcal R}_n=\\mathcal R_n,\n \\qquad\n \\widetilde{\\mathcal C}_n^h=\\mathcal C_n^h.\n\\]\nFor every law in the compact class, every coordinate interval then has exactly the width of its deterministic-\\(K_\\Sigma\\) counterpart with probability one; consequently the coordinatewise no-wider probability equals one for every \\(n\\), and hence tends to one for every interior regular law in that class. The moment region has finite-sample coverage at least \\(1-\\alpha\\) uniformly over the compact class, its endpoints are rationally outward rounded, and its exact branch-free semialgebraic inverse is unchanged. This answer certifies equality of widths and does not certify a strict adaptive improvement.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "CalHandle",
      "Ksigma",
      "Cmoment",
      "n",
      "alpha",
      "Rinverse",
      "Ch"
    ],
